{
  "title": "Brexit Bill Sparks Outcry: Are Animals Recognized as Sentient in the UK?",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "type": "lead",
      "content": "A firestorm erupted last week after a Commons vote on an amendment to the European Union (Withdrawal) Bill led to widespread accusations that British MPs no longer recognized animals as sentient beings. The controversial vote, which saw the amendment defeated by a narrow margin of 313 to 295, has triggered a fierce debate and drawn sharp criticism from animal rights groups and public figures alike."
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "content": "The amendment sought to directly transfer the EU protocol on animal sentience into UK law. However, immediately following the vote on November 15th, a defensive pushback began from Conservative MPs. Tory MP Zac Goldsmith dismissed some of the reporting as 'weird and dishonest,' clarifying via Twitter that the vote was about the 'correct legal procedure for ensuring animal welfare standards – not on the principle of whether animals are sentient.' Fellow Conservative Rachel Maclean, MP for Redditch, echoed this sentiment in a video, decrying 'fake news' and asserting that 'This government, and in fact all governments, are deeply committed to continuing to protect animals as sentient beings. That law is already written into our own law.'"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "content": "Prime Minister Theresa May further solidified the government's stance at Wednesday’s PMQs, stating unequivocally: 'We recognise and respect that animals are sentient beings, and should be treated accordingly.' In a bid to quell the growing public anger, Tory MP Hugo Swire republished a letter he received from Environment Secretary Michael Gove on social media, adding his personal conviction: 'I do not believe that any MP thinks that animals do not have feelings or emotions.'"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "content": "Despite these reassurances, the vote has ignited protests across the UK. Animal rights groups have launched multiple online petitions targeting the House of Lords, UK parliament, and Michael Gove. Prominent public figures, including presenter Sue Perkins, have voiced strong criticism. Gudrun Ravetz, senior vice-president of the British Veterinary Association, expressed profound concern, stating: 'It is extremely concerning that a marginal majority of MPs have voted-down this seminal clause. This action undermines the government’s previous promises that the UK will continue to be known for our high standards of animal health and welfare post-Brexit.'"
    },
    {
      "type": "body",
      "content": "Conversely, Conservative MP for Crawley, Henry Smith, offered a different perspective, pointing out that even under existing EU law, many practices opposed by animal rights groups are still permitted across Europe. The controversial amendment, spearheaded by Green party co-leader Caroline Lucas among others, aimed to directly incorporate Article 13 of Title II of the Lisbon Treaty into UK law. During the debate last Wednesday, Lucas highlighted the historical context, noting that the origins of the clause could be traced back to the 1997 UK presidency of the EU, and that since 1999, EU law had explicitly recognized animals as sentient life forms."
    }
  ]
}
```

